Breeding out of natural habitats contributes to the survival of the most endangered species as two juveniles successfully hatch artificially
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, said that the breeding of whispers outside the natural habitat contributes to the survival of this endangered species.
Two whiskey eggs were recently collected from a whiskey sanctuary in the lowlands north of the Tonle Sap Lake. Collected egg whites were sent to the Whistle Breeding Center at the Angkor Center for Biodiversity Conservation (ACCB), where the eggs were successfully artificially hatched.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra also congratulated the Angkor Center for Biodiversity Conservation (ACCB) experts on the successful artificial hatching of two whiskey eggs collected from lowland whiskey conservation areas. North of Tonle Sap Lake. This is really great news for conservatives and the Cambodian people.
"The two hatchlings hatched in the first week of May and the two hatchlings were fed by our experienced veterinarians," the spokesman said. They will be included in the ACCB Whistleblower Population Population Report, where they will contribute to the survival of this endangered species.
