(Phnom Penh): Mr. Wasim Sorya, Under Secretary of State, on behalf of Ms. Imnithara, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, on May 22, 2023, presided over the opening ceremony of the Consultative Workshop and Review of the Training Needs Assessment Report of the Capacity Building Project on Road maintenance approaches to promote sustainable infrastructure for Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam.
The workshop was also attended by relevant units under the auspices of the Ministry and the Department of Public Works and Transport of Cambodia, the Ministry of Transport of Vietnam, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport of Laos and representatives of relevant associations, a total of 60 participants.
The purpose of this workshop is to help review, provide input and provide additional feedback on the results of the training needs assessment in order to improve and improve the project needs assessment report of this project. In addition, the talks also provided an opportunity for the three countries to learn from each other, which is another important part that will bring benefits to both the national and regional levels.
Speaking on that occasion, Mr. Wasim Sorya also congratulated and praised the Techo Sen Institute of Public Works and Transport for its active efforts in both core work and seeking support from national and international partners in the direction towards The joint vision of the institute itself, as well as that of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, aims to "build and strengthen human resources in the public sector and transport."
Mr. Wasim Sorya added that this workshop is a testament to another new achievement of the Institute, which will lead to the organization of technical training on road maintenance methods not only for Cambodia but also for Vietnam and Laos. Well.
It should also be noted that the capacity building project on the road maintenance method was implemented by the Training and Training Department of Techo Sen Institute of Public Works and Transport, which received budget approval from the Mekong-Republic of Korea Cooperation Fund (MKCF). ) Through the Mekong Institute for 24 months.
