Korean government donates nearly $ 110,000 in ophthalmology medical equipment to National Children's Hospital
(Phnom Penh): The Government of the Republic of Korea, through the Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA-KOICA), has donated nearly $ 110,000 in modern ophthalmology medical equipment to the National Children's Hospital to improve the eye health of Cambodian infants and children. Even better.
The handover ceremony took place on May 22, 2023 at the National Children's Hospital under the presence of Professor Nheb Angka Bos, Director of the National Children's Hospital and a representative of KOICA.
Professor Nhep Angka-bos said the ophthalmology equipment, valued at nearly $ 110,000, was donated by the Korea International Cooperation Agency for screening newborns with disabilities and children to adults.
He added that through this ophthalmology medical equipment, the National Children's Hospital will launch eye examinations for infants, children and the elderly to improve the eye health of Cambodian infants and children.
He added that this material is important for immediate eye examination of newborns with special problems, especially premature babies, most of whom have cataracts. "In the past, we have had cataracts in newborns. Our challenge is to find a place to provide services, if any, to be sent away, so this is another development of the hospital," he said. Our national children.
"When the hospital opens its pediatric ophthalmology service, it will help infants and children, especially premature infants with cataracts, to receive effective treatment to avoid disability," he added. . "Apart from this, there are other modern devices for scanning and measuring cataracts, as well as infants or children with cataracts and provide medical services as well as minor surgeries."
According to the plan, the hospital will gradually start providing pediatric eye care services in 2023, after the hospital receives this modern medical equipment from KOICA, and next month the hospital will send this eye specialist. Continue a month-and-a-half study in the Republic of Korea to learn more about new techniques for effective eye examination and surgery for infants and children.
He said the KOICA grant was also part of the Korean government's project to help build a five-story, more than 8,300-square-meter building that is scheduled to be completed by mid-2024 and completed in three to three years. The next half, at a cost of about $ 16 million.
He added that when the new building is completed, pediatric ophthalmology services will be installed in the building, equipped with more modern medical equipment for general ophthalmology and eye surgery.
