The Senior Minister confirmed to Fresh News, "This is a historical project from the Funan period to Samdech Techo, which we have restored to use the existing system of Funan for our national interest to transport goods to the sea directly without crossing. To neighboring countries. This project is very important, very important.
At the same time, after the news was spread, people on social media also expressed their enthusiasm and happiness for this project. They consider this project a giant project, a historical project in the field of transportation in Cambodia.
It should be noted that finally, the project to dig a waterway connecting the river to the sea of Cambodia, which is an important project for the transportation of goods, has been approved by the Royal Government to open a more in-depth study with a budget of about 1.7 billion. Dollars and will take 4 years. This decision was made at a cabinet meeting chaired by Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen this morning, Friday, May 19, 2023.
Prior to the approval of this project, Samdech Techo and the leaders of the Royal Government had a very careful debate. In particular, listen to the explanations from Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, who is studying and implementing this project.
Discussions took place from 10 a.m. to more than 1 p.m. With this approval, Samdech Techo made many recommendations related to the environmental impact caused by the sea and continue to discuss with the Joint Committee of the Mekong River Commission.
The project is named "Bassac River Navigation and Logistics Project". The Tonle Bassac Navigation and Logistics System Project aims to expand the potential use of water transport to contribute to the promotion of socio-economic activities to a higher level and to provide appropriate solutions to Challenges and obstacles of the water transport sector that have been facing today.
The technical working group set the goal of this study to increase the sovereignty of Cambodia's inland waterway transport, reduce the time, distance and budget, reduce the procedures for ships and inbound and outbound, make it profitable and accelerate the national economic development. Inland waterway integration through the expansion of multi-modal transport links, reducing road damage and traffic congestion and reducing road accidents.
Studies by technical teams have also shown that similar waterways connecting rivers to the sea have been excavated since the Funan Empire, and that waterways will be built on waterways. Antiquities left behind.
The project has been studied in great detail in terms of technology, legal aspects, impacts and economic benefits. The study is detailed below:
General technical characteristics of the waterway: According to the study, this waterway is about 180 km long, starting from the Takeo canal of the Mekong River, passing through the Ta Ek canal of the Bassac River, then entering the Ta Hing canal of the Bassac River, Koh Thom district and continuing until Sea Kep, passing through four provinces: Kandal, Takeo, Kampot and Kep, with a total population of 1.6 million people living on either side of this waterway.
* Engineering and architectural design of waterway infrastructure, waterway construction and waterway valves: The construction of this waterway embankment will use two appropriate methods: planting grass in navigable canals and preparing the banks of the Bassac River at the point of the canal. Ta Ek to Prek Ta Hing using Geotextile and Geobag sandbags to prevent landslides.
The project will build three navigation valves in Kandal, Takeo and Kep provinces, and 11 bridges.
* Results of hydrological analysis and flow, glacial water and saline infiltration: This project will require an average of less than 2% of water from natural rivers and streams under normal hydrological conditions.
* Results of environmental and ecological analysis and evaluation: During the implementation of the construction project, there may be minimal environmental impact, but it can be managed through the application of environmental and waste management rules in accordance with existing national and international standards. Already implemented some of the Ministry's infrastructure development projects effectively.
Pursuant to Article 5 of the 1995 Mekong Agreement on Reasonable and Equitable Water Use in their respective territories, this project can be implemented by the Cambodian side only with prior notice to the Joint Committee of the River Commission. Mekong attached with a detailed study report prior to the commencement of construction work, without the need for any approval from the Mekong River Commission member countries.
* Results of economic analysis, investment and calculation of economic benefits: The new waterway project is estimated to cost about US $ 1.7 billion and take about four years to build.
The project will reduce the current time and distance of transit through the Vietnamese port of Kaim, about 69 km from Phnom Penh Autonomous Port to Sihanoukville Port and about 135 km from Phnom Penh Autonomous Port to Port. Kampot Province.
