Nurses in South Korea strike after Yun rejects pay demands for better working conditions
SEOUL: Thousands of nurses in South Korea went on strike on Friday after President Yoon Suk Yeol rejected a draft law to improve salaries and working conditions in a timely manner. There have been protests from doctors and nursing assistants who fear the law will affect their work. This is according to the publication by the AFP news agency on Friday, May 19, 2023.
The bill passed the opposition-led parliament in April after protests by some medical staff that the new law would open the door for nurses to provide treatment without a medical license. Nurses say doctors' claims are baseless and that South Korea needs more care centers to cope with the aging population.
The impact of the strike has been seen to be limited so far, as most protesters have taken time off or reduced working hours, with major hospitals operating normally. South Korean Health Minister Cho Kyoo-hong chaired an emergency response meeting and urged hospitals to monitor the situation closely to prevent strikes from affecting patients.
Rejecting the law, Yoon said the new law would cause "excessive controversy" among medical staff and the practice of nursing outside medical institutions, which would raise public concern about the health care system.
