Please be informed that the Digital Skills Development (DWD) Project is a five-year project implemented in partnership with the University of California, Berkeley to strengthen educational outcomes for Cambodia's youth and professionals in the growing digital economy. Universal.
Scholarships are designed for multi-ethnic, talented, under-represented students to increase access to quality digital related certifications, along with associate and undergraduate programs, in line with partner skills requirements. In the private sector.
In addition to the 18 scholarships awarded today, a total of 74 DWD scholarships were awarded to students from three universities, including the National University of Battambang, Svay Rieng University and the National University of Management in Phnom Penh, and 65% of Scholarships are awarded to female students across the country.
During the USAID-DWD Scholarship students' study program, they will receive training support from the network of private companies and partner universities and through other job center activities such as vocational training. Software, communication programs, internships and job fairs.
She added that the job center will act as a bridge connecting employers to talented individuals in Cambodia through various fields in a central location at the university.
The Job Center of the National University of Battambang is the fifth job center of the USAID-DWD project, which has been launched, helping to ensure the sustainability of the achievements of digital employment skills development.
These centers will continue to build industry relationships and build long-term skills after the completion of the USAID-DWD project.
