The 37-year-old man was executed in Changi Prison in eastern Singapore. The man, a Singaporean of Malay descent, was convicted in 2019 of smuggling 1.5 kilograms of marijuana. On April 26, Singapore hanged a 46-year-old man for smuggling more than one kilogram of marijuana.
Under Singaporean law, smuggling more than 500 grams of marijuana carries the death penalty. After being suspended during the 199 Kovid Outbreak, Singapore suspended a total of 11 prisoners in 2022, all of them involved in drug offenses.
Under international human rights law, countries that uphold the death penalty are limited to the most serious crimes, such as intentional homicide. But Singapore's Interior Ministry argues that the death penalty is an important component of Singapore's criminal justice system and is effective in keeping Singapore safe and secure.
