Currently, Cambodia has three rare and endangered species: wild ox, big tiger and rhinoceros.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that under the care of stakeholders, after Cambodia achieved peace, some endangered species were bred, including: mountain crocodile or fish crocodile, frog-headed tortoise , Royal turtles, whispers or peacocks to no longer return to nature.
Meanwhile, a spokesman for the Ministry of Environment wants to see more endangered species being bred to avoid extinction and rehabilitate them. What we have lost and we are trying to restore when other countries have a species, we can ask, but if that species is extinct in the world, how can we restore that species? ? ”
Mr. Neth Pheaktra continued that what we all strive to do within the framework of the Zero Trap Campaign is to restore rare species, provide safe habitat and improve techniques and methods to increase the distribution of rare wildlife. In natural habitats.
At the same time, Mr. Pheaktra stated that these points are an effort to ensure that these rare species are preserved and not only for the benefit of the Cambodian people, but for humanity and for the ecosystem. The whole thing at once.
During a press conference on Trap Zero in Khemarak Phoumin City, Koh Kong Province on May 15, 2023, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said: "We have to increase and not wait until we lose it. We take care of it. Think about getting this species back. We face a lot of problems."
The spokesman for the Ministry of Environment also emphasized that the program, which the Ministry of Environment, together with partner organizations and provincial administrations in which the Natural Resource Protection Zone has launched the Zero Trap Campaign, not only removes and eliminates traps in natural forests, but also changes The attitude of the people not to eat wild animals but to give hope to other rare species that are facing extinction.
Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, said that to increase wildlife populations and prevent the extinction of wildlife caused by trapping and hunting requires an unprecedented change in conservation strategies and interventions through Highly innovative modern approach.
"We need to provide a safe haven for wildlife, and it is also a place where biodiversity is cut off from all threats to human activity, as well as an area with high management efficiency," he said. "
"Stopping hunting, trapping wildlife and switching to wildlife protection is the right choice for Cambodia," he said. The Zero Trap Campaign is a great and timely reminder to change the mindset of all Cambodians to join in rescuing and conserving our country's rare and precious wildlife. "
