Government officials and conservationists are working together to eradicate all forms of natural resource crime and create jobs for local communities.
(Koh Kong): The second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign, which will continue to reach the southwestern provinces of Cambodia, reflects the strong commitment of all of us to eliminate all types of traps in Cambodia's protected areas. Stop the illegal wildlife trade along with the creation of livelihoods instead to support the economic development of our local community.
While the first phase of the Zero Trap campaign was successfully completed and achieved proud results in the provinces of Mondulkiri, Ratanakiri, Kratie, Stung Treng, Kampong Thom and Preah Vihear in 2022.
The Zero Trap Campaign is designed to raise public awareness of the importance of wildlife, their role in ecosystems, the challenges of the trap crisis, and call on relevant institutions, local authorities, the private sector and local people to become more active. Protecting wildlife and managing natural resources for future generations.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the Ministry of Environment has encouraged the active participation of all relevant institutions, including sub-national and provincial authorities in strengthening the implementation of laws that support the protection of wildlife and encourage Local stakeholders and other communities in wildlife conservation to develop ecotourism.
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, emphasized that “increasing wildlife populations and preventing the extinction of wildlife species caused by trapping and hunting requires unprecedented changes in conservation strategies and interventions. "Modern, highly innovative approach."
"We need to provide a safe haven for wildlife, and it is also a place where biodiversity is cut off from all threats to human activity, as well as an area that is highly effective management," he added. . Many conservation partners have highlighted the importance of protecting Cambodia's forests and wildlife.
Tuy Serey Vathana, Program Director of the Wildlife and Nature Coalition, continued: "Stopping the habit of setting dangerous traps will bring long-term benefits to wildlife, habitats and people. "Together we protect Cambodia's great biodiversity and improve the lives of communities and public health, which makes a positive difference."
