The World Health Organization (WHO) says it applauds the potential and growth of AI, but is concerned about how it can be used to improve access to health information as a decision-making tool and to improve diagnostic care.
The WHO says it is excited about the potential and advancement of AI, but is concerned about how it will be used to improve access to health information as a decision-making tool and to improve diagnostic care.
The data used to train AI may be biased and misleading or inaccurate, and the model may be misused to create false information, the WHO said in a statement.
The warning comes at a time when artificial intelligence is rapidly gaining popularity, highlighting technologies that can support business and social processes.
