During the 2023 spawning season at the Koh Kong Pottery Conservation Center, 21 nests of turtles were found (from January 15 to February 26, 2022) with a total of 272 eggs on a nearby artificial beach. Breeding pond in the pottery conservation center of Koh Kong province.
The hatching of the baby turtles demonstrates the importance of establishing a breeding ground for endangered species in the country of origin. Adolescent turtles in the wild in Cambodia are few in number, so breeding turtles in the center is the best option, as we not only guarantee the survival of the species in the country, but also increase the number of turtle species. In natural habitats as well.
According to Mr. Thorn Phon, Coordinator of the Royal Turtle and Crocodile Project in Koh Kong Province, for the breeding of royal turtles in 2023 is successful because 122 royal turtles have successfully hatched and hope that this year Going forward, the number of royal turtles will increase by 200.
The expert also expressed concern about a number of factors that threaten the turtles in natural canals due to the illegal use of fishing gear. In particular, mosquito nets have killed many turtles, while in the last five years there have been at least 10 turtles, most of whom have died from nets.
Illegal fishing, habitat loss, sand exploitation and deforestation in the Sre Sre Ambel canal system in Koh Kong and Sihanoukville provinces have affected the habitat and breeding of these turtles. It is found only in the Prek Sre Ambel canal system in Koh Kong and Sihanoukville provinces in Cambodia, which are facing extinction.
On the occasion of leading a group of journalists to visit the artificial beach in Koh Kong Provincial Reptile Conservation Center on May 15, 2023, Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, praised all relevant institutions. Submitted to protect and conserve the Royal Turtle, an endangered species in Cambodia.
The spokesman recalled that the royal tortoise was initially believed to have disappeared from Cambodia, but in 2000 the royal tortoise was rediscovered until experts took the tortoise to Breeding and no longer return to the natural canal.
With the efforts to increase the number of turtles, Mr. Net Pheaktra called on all people and fishermen to participate in the protection and conservation of this species of turtle, because this turtle is very valuable in the world. There are only three countries where the royal tortoise is present: Cambodia, Indonesia and Malaysia.
