Japan provides 10 ambulances and other relief equipment to the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Government and people of Japan, through the project "Building a Sustainable Health Care System During the Kovid-19 Outbreak by Improving Life-Saving Services and Using Digital Systems in Cambodia" 10 ambulances, 140 midwives, 10 maternity pumps and 400 shock-absorbing pants for the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia.
The handover ceremony was presided over by Dr. Yuk Sambath, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Health, Mr. Ou Nou Atsushi, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to Cambodia, and Ms. Sandra Burnlau, Country Representative of the United Nations Population Fund. (UNFPA) in Cambodia and many other health officials this morning, May 12, 2023 at the National Center for Maternal and Child Health Chroy Changvar.
The Secretary of State stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia, under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, has achieved great achievements in all fields, especially peacekeeping, political stability, security, order and social security, control of communicable diseases. -19.
"On behalf of the Ministry of Health and myself, I would like to thank the people and government of Japan for providing timely financial assistance to support the Ministry of Health for building resilient health care systems, especially during outbreaks. Of Kovid-19 disease.
She added that the funding focused on the provision of medical equipment to health facilities for the provision of emergency maternity and neonatal care. In addition, the grant supports the development of e-learning materials for health professionals to provide reproductive health, sexual and maternal health services, especially for vulnerable people after the Kovid-19 outbreak.
Access to ambulances and life-saving medical equipment to help strengthen the health care system and provide the highest quality care allows communities to access appropriate health services, especially for women and newborns in the northeastern provinces of Cambodia, as a region, needs equipment to save the lives of mothers and newborns in the event of a dangerous situation.
This humanitarian assistance contributes to the Royal Government of Cambodia's commitment in Nairobi in 2019 to eliminate preventable maternal mortality by 2030.
Ambulances will be used to increase access to life-saving services and timely interventions for women and newborns and emergencies, she said.
Maternity pumps are used to save the lives of mothers and babies during prolonged difficult labor, while midwifery kits include safety devices that midwives use during childbirth and shock absorbers are new tools to prevent and reduce Bleeding during childbirth These devices are absolutely essential in saving lives.
The Ministry of Health would like to thank the Government of Japan for its generous and timely support to the Ministry of Health through the United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA).
During Kovid-19, the Ministry of Health introduced a series of necessary measures to ensure the provision of essential health services, including reproductive, sexual and maternal health services. The Ministry of Health has prepared guidelines for the provision of maternal and child health services during the global epidemic of Kovid-19 in early 2020 through technical assistance from UNFPA.
This guideline is an important document to ensure the continued provision of reproductive health, sexual and maternal health services during the global epidemic of Kovid-19. UNFPA has provided technical assistance to build a digital platform for e-learning for health professionals, providing remote services as part of strengthening the health system in response to Kovid-19 disease.
Taking this opportunity, the Permanent Secretary of State praised the United Nations Population Fund, a longtime partner of the Ministry of Health of Cambodia for its nearly 30 years of support. Through this partnership, UNFPA has contributed to strengthening reproductive health services, sexual health and maternal health, including family planning, women's well-being, to reduce maternal mortality in Cambodia.
This year marks the 70th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations between Japan and Cambodia. The Government of Japan has been supporting the Royal Government's efforts to save the lives of mothers, babies and improve the health of children in Cambodia for a long time.
At the same time, she would like to express her deep gratitude to the Government and people of Japan, especially Mr. Uno Atsushi, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia, for always facilitating the smooth running of aid projects, especially in these circumstances. Of the fight against Kovid-19.
