ASEAN leaders call on Myanmar to end all forms of violence
(Indonesia): ASEAN leaders have reiterated their call for Myanmar to end all forms of violence and to condemn attacks on humanitarian activities by ASEAN officials in the country.
The call was made at the ASEAN Leaders' Summit on the morning of May 11, 2023 in Labun Bajo, Indonesia.
At the summit, ASEAN leaders issued a joint statement reiterating Myanmar's call for an end to all forms of violence. ASEAN leaders also expressed support for the Indonesian president's statement criticizing the attack on ASEAN humanitarian aid workers in Myanmar.
In a statement issued on May 10 after the 42nd summit, ASEAN leaders also endorsed the Indonesian president's statement and urged those who attack humanitarian officials to take responsibility for their actions. They.
ASEAN leaders have expressed grave concern over the violence in Myanmar and called for a ceasefire in all its forms and the use of force to create a safe environment and provide timely humanitarian assistance.
The ASEAN leaders reaffirmed their support for the ASEAN Chair in maintaining relations with all stakeholders in Myanmar and encouraged the implementation of the five points of consensus on Myanmar issues. Outcome of the 42nd ASEAN Summit in Labun Bajo, Indonesia
During the two-day meeting, ASEAN leaders welcomed the first participation of the Prime Minister of Timor-Leste in the ASEAN Leaders' Meeting on the occasion of the 42nd ASEAN Summit. 11 of ASEAN.
ASEAN leaders reaffirmed their commitment to further strengthen the ASEAN Community, ASEAN Centrality and Unity, and ASEAN External Relations.
At the summit, ASEAN leaders adopted the following declarations and statements:
ASEAN Leaders also reaffirmed their commitment to the full and effective implementation of the ASEAN Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (AEC) in all aspects, as well as the establishment of the Provisional ASP Secretariat as a support unit at the ASEAN Secretariat. Become a separate Asean Secretariat to strengthen the supply chain in the region and contribute to the ASEAN Economic Integration Agenda as well as maintain the ASEAN Community within the ASEAN Club.
The ASEAN Socio-Cultural Community is committed to making ASEAN the center of growth by increasing investment in human resource development to be more competitive and sharp towards a future of work and ready for the digital transformation. Fast, fast.
ASEAN leaders also reaffirmed their continued support for Myanmar's efforts to bring peace and harmony among the communities involved and to promote sustainable and sustainable development in Rakhine State.
On external relations, ASEAN leaders stressed the importance of ASEAN centrality and unity in engaging with external partners through the ASEAN Plus One, the ASEAN Plus Three, the East Asia Summit, the ASEAN Regional Forum and the ASEAN Defense Ministers' Meeting. Plus.
ASEAN leaders also discussed regional and international issues, including the South China Sea, the Korean Peninsula, the Middle East, Myanmar and the war in Ukraine.
It should be noted that in this 42nd Summit, ASEAN leaders also met with representatives of the ASEAN Inter-Parliamentary Assembly, ASEAN Youth Representatives, ASEAN Business Council Representatives, the High-Level Working Group on the ASEAN Community Post-2025 Vision.
In the meeting with the representatives, they discussed the challenges that the sectors of the Inter-Parliamentary Assembly, Youth Representatives and the private sector across ASEAN are facing.
At the same time, representatives of all the Advisory Councils, including the ASEAN Business Section, submitted reports, recommendations and progress on the work of the 10 ASEAN Leaders. In particular, promoting stronger regional economic integration. The ASEAN Business Advisory Council was established in 2001 by the ASEAN Heads of State / Government.
After the first day of the summit, ASEAN leaders took a cruise to watch the sunset over the sea of Labunbacho, East Nusa Tenggara province. ASEAN leaders expressed their cordiality at the outcome of the 42nd ASEAN Summit, which led to the issuance of the ASEAN Chair Statement.
Then, on the night of May 10, Indonesian President Joko Widodo and his wife hosted a banquet in honor of the ASEAN Heads of State and Government who attended the 42nd ASEAN Summit in Labour, Bajo. Indonesia.
President Joko Widodo expressed his deep gratitude to all ASEAN leaders for attending and attending the 42nd ASEAN Summit in Labour Bajo.
On the morning of May 11, ASEAN leaders attended the 42nd ASEAN Summit. At the summit, the leaders issued a joint statement calling for a renewed call for Myanmar to end all forms of violence. ASEAN leaders also expressed support for the Indonesian president's statement criticizing the attack on ASEAN humanitarian aid workers in Myanmar.
ASEAN leaders, in a statement issued on May 10 after the 42nd summit, supported the Indonesian president's statement and urged the perpetrators to be held accountable. ASEAN leaders, meanwhile, reiterated Myanmar's call for an end to all forms of violence to restore peace in the country.
ASEAN leaders have expressed grave concern over the violence in Myanmar and called for a ceasefire in all its forms and the use of force to create a safe environment and provide timely humanitarian assistance.
In addition, the ASEAN leaders reaffirmed their support for the ASEAN Chair in maintaining relations with all stakeholders in Myanmar and encouraged the implementation of the five-point consensus. Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit lead a delegation to return to Cambodia after the meeting
