Japan provides nearly $ 70,000 to support observers for the 2023 parliamentary elections
(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan, through the Embassy of Cambodia, has provided US $ 69,333 to a Recipient under the Small-Scale Human Security Project (Kusanoni) for Supporting Election Observation Election of the 7th legislature in 2023.
The Grant Agreement was signed on May 10, 2023 by Mr. Uno Atsushi, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to the Kingdom of Cambodia and a representative of the Recipient Institution. This funding is provided for the project to support election observation for the parliamentary elections. Recipient Institution: Committee for Free and Fair Elections in Cambodia (CPP).
Ueno Atsushi briefly explained Japan's support for democracy in Cambodia. Japan has always supported and accompanied Cambodia on the path of democratic development since before the achievement of peace in Cambodia in the early 1990s.
The Japanese ambassador said that the Japanese government has paid close attention to supporting the election, which is the foundation of democracy, and has continued to support the election through various means, both direct and indirect, including the sending of observers. Vote for Cambodia for the last general election.
For the CPP, the Japanese government has supported four national and local elections since the 2002 Commune / Sangkat Council elections. The CPP has developed election handbooks, legal handouts, leaflets for voters through these projects, and I understand that these materials have contributed to raising awareness of the proper electoral process.
The presence of election observation by various actors is an important element in holding free and fair elections. With this in mind, the Japanese government has been supporting a number of projects through the Kusanoni grant to ensure free and fair elections with organizations, including the NEC, which work on election propaganda and education. Vote.
Under this project, printed T-shirts and materials will be distributed to 10,241 short-term election observers who will be deployed throughout Cambodia during the parliamentary elections this month. This coming July. We expect this project to contribute to ensuring free and fair elections and respect for the will of all voters, including women, youth and vulnerable groups.
Japan's Kusanoni Grant began in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level.
The grant aims to protect vulnerable people from a number of factors, such as poverty and / or misfortune, that could directly threaten their lives, livelihoods and dignity, as well as promote their self-reliance. Communities. Since 1991, the Government of Japan has provided more than $ 66 million to local authorities and NGOs to implement more than 680 Kusanoni projects throughout Cambodia.
