Joint Press Release: Ending the Trap Crisis, Wildlife Trafficking and Eating All Kinds of Wildlife in Cambodia Effectively Requires the Involvement of All Stakeholders
(Kampong Speu): On May 9, 2023, the "Zero Trap" campaign in the second phase of the protected area was held in Kampong Speu, the second province after Pursat, with the initiative of the Ministry of Environment and Environment. Collaborate with partner organizations in the field of conservation in Cambodia.
Kampong Speu is a province with potential in the field of ecotourism, where local people living in some protected areas have developed ecotourism sites to participate in the conservation of natural resources and create a local economy for communities living in and near those areas. Relying on wildlife trapping, illegal wildlife trade, facing prosecution and catastrophic loss of natural resources.
The Zero Trap Campaign in Kampong Speu was attended by more than 500 stakeholders from government institutions, the private sector, civil society organizations and people from all walks of life, and was committed to stopping hunting and trafficking in wildlife. Commit to some model restaurants without supplying or cooking wildlife meat to their customers.
According to a report from the Kampong Speu Provincial Department of Environment, in 2022, environmental officials and rangers have been working to strengthen law enforcement by patrolling forests and collecting traps in protected areas and biodiversity corridors. 379 traps, especially in 2023 from January to April, a total of 382 traps collected.
As individuals, we can make a difference by raising awareness and promoting the impact of anti-wildlife trapping on wildlife, supporting conservation efforts, and advocating for implementation. Stricter laws against the illegal trade in these wildlife. "This is a time when we must take action and protect our precious wildlife before it is too late."
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, emphasized that "the commitment of restaurant owners in the provinces that have been the target of zero trapping campaigns in the past has demonstrated an understanding of the value and role of wildlife in the wild."
Kampong Speu Provincial Governor Calls for Elimination of Wildlife Hunting and Supports Strict Law Enforcement for Individuals Destroying Natural Resources
(Kampong Speu): Mr. Vy Samnang, Governor of Kampong Speu Province, called for the elimination of wildlife hunting, wildlife trafficking, eating all kinds of wildlife meat and support the strict implementation of the law for individuals who commit crimes against natural resources.
The provincial governor also praised all relevant officials who actively participated in law enforcement to crack down on the activities of criminals who often steal natural resources in their jurisdiction.
The provincial governor emphasized that "the perpetrators have violated the forest law, the law on the protection of natural resources is very strict, without exception, because this trap not only kills animals, but also kills people." "The livelihood of the people is just right."
At the same time, Mr. Vy Samnang said that if there is a lack of participation from the people, wildlife and forests will be destroyed, so please participate in protecting and conserving natural resources together. "Because we have seen more clearly the destruction of natural resources that the world is getting back to the punishment of nature."
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, “Kampong Speu is a risk area related to wildlife meat, in which we see wildlife meat sold in restaurants on the streets and food stalls in tourist areas. Although wildlife dishes are not included in the menu, we still see the introduction of wild game dishes such as wild boar or coyote to customers, often by word of mouth. "
