Mr. Mean Sati said that election observers include:
* National observers are Cambodians or foreigners who are members of local associations and non-governmental organizations registered with the Ministry of Interior and authorized by the NEC.
* International observers are observers of international non-governmental organizations, international organizations and countries invited and recognized by the Royal Government of Cambodia and authorized by the NEC.
* National Mobile Observer refers to the leaders or officials of national associations or non-governmental organizations whose application is approved and approved by the NEC and allowed to observe the election process throughout the country.
Regarding the role of observers, Mr. Mean Sati stated that observers have the right to conduct independent election observation activities. National observers may only visit the capitals and provinces requested by the NGO or association, except for mobile national observers throughout the country who are authorized and certified by the NEC.
He added that international observers can travel anywhere in the territory of the Kingdom of Cambodia without the need for permission or prior notice, except for obvious reasons related to personal security. In case of any emergency or insecurity, the observer must inform the election officials and the relevant local authorities to help solve it in time.
Mr. Mean Sati stated that the rights, duties and responsibilities of election observers and the prohibitions on election observers are set out in the Code of Conduct for Election Observers in the election process.
Involving international observers in monitoring the election of NEC representatives is considered an important task to reflect the accuracy of the election process to some extent. However, the law does not stipulate that voting is considered invalid without the participation of election observers. Election observation is voluntary, however, the NEC has called on national and international observers to observe the election.
