IMF raises forecast for Asia as China recovers
NEW YORK (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) this week raised its forecast for Asia's economy as China's recovery boosted growth but warned of chronic inflation risks and global market volatility. Driven by the banking crisis in the West.
Reuters quoted the IMF as saying that China's economic recovery will be important for Asia, which will see a focus on consumption and demand in services rather than investment.
"Asia and the Pacific will be the most energy-intensive of the world's major regions by 2023, largely driven by vision," the IMF said in its Asia Vision report. "Strong for China and India."
Asia's economy is expected to grow 4.6 percent this year after 3.8 percent in 2022, accounting for about 70 percent of global growth, according to the IMF. The forecast was 0.3 percentage points higher than in October.
