244 Blue Hat Troops and Two Dogs Will Go on a Humanitarian Mission in Central Africa Tonight (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): For the past 17 years, the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen has Continue to send nearly 9,000 Cambodian Blue Hat troops on peacekeeping missions under the United Nations umbrella in countries devastated by war and chronic internal conflicts that have achieved great prestige for the nation.
As an encouragement, on May 05, 2023, Cambodia organized a ceremony to send 244 people of the 423th Special Engineering Squadron, 30 women and 2 hitmin dogs before leaving for peacekeeping missions. Under the United Nations umbrella in the Central African Republic (MINUSCA).
The farewell ceremony was presided over by General Pol Saroeun, Senior Minister in Charge of the United Nations Cambodian Peacekeeping Force, Joseph Scheuer, UN Resident Coordinator in Cambodia, and General Sem Sovanny. Director of the National Center for Peacekeeping Forces Management, as well as leaders of several national centers.
General Sem Sovanny said that the 423rd Engineering Brigade, the ninth brigade ready to fly to the Central African Republic, had a total of 244 personnel, including 30 women from 40 units of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces. The forces that will go on missions in this year 9 have all received quality pre-mobile and professional training from Cambodian trainers and from friendly countries for the same efficiency and effectiveness as the previous divisions.
Senior Minister Pol Saroeun is pleased that Cambodia has sent nearly 9,000 troops to the United Nations peacekeeping mission, with a series of major achievements since 2006.
On the occasion of the 17th anniversary of Cambodia's deployment of troops under the auspices of the United Nations, Senior Minister Pol Saroeun sincerely appreciated and appreciated the sacrifices of Cambodian peacekeepers.
Senior Minister Pol Saroeun also instructed all army chiefs to adhere to and strictly implement the four-point statement of Samdech Te Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
At the same time, Joseph Scheuer expressed his gratitude and pride to Cambodia for its strong commitment to peacekeeping operations. In particular, UN figures show that by the end of 2022, Cambodia ranked fifth in the world in terms of the number of women deployed out of 78 countries donating force.
