Uncertainty over Japan's monetary policy could spread around the world
NEW YORK (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) on Thursday warned of "uncertainty" over Japan's monetary policy direction, saying possible changes from the lowest interest rates could have a significant impact on the economy. Global financial markets.
Reuters reports that Krishna Srinivasan, head of the IMF's Asia and Pacific division, pointed to risks surrounding Asia's economic outlook, including declining exports to advanced economies, declining productivity in the region, Reuters reported. In China and the split in world trade.
"In the medium term, we expect the Chinese economy to experience a decline in productivity and investment, which will slow economic growth to below 4 percent by 2028," he said.
While most Asian central banks continue to tighten monetary policy, Japan remains an exception, with Japanese inflation still moderate, although this may change.
"Balancing these investors' investment portfolios could lead to an increase in global output, causing portfolio outflows for some countries," he said.
