Myanmar's military junta on Wednesday announced it would pardon more than 2,000 prisoners being held on charges of inciting anti-military insurgency. This was released by the AFP news agency on Wednesday, May 3, 2023.
In a statement, the Myanmar military regime stated that the military would pardon 2,153 prisoners incarcerated under Article 505 of the Penal Code to celebrate the Vesak festival, and that the amnesty was for peace. The minds of the people of Myanmar will stand on humanitarian principles. However, those who are released and commit crimes again will be sentenced to the remaining prison terms with additional penalties.
More than 21,000 people have been detained since Myanmar's military coup overthrew Aung San Suu Kyi's civilian government in February 2021, leaving the Southeast Asian country in a state of disarray. The unrest of violence to this day. According to the United Nations, at least 170 journalists are being held in prisons.
