(Phnom Penh): On the occasion of the 137th Anniversary of the International Labor Day on May 1, the tripartite government, trade unions and employers, as well as the International Labor Organization (ILO), all noted the progress of industrial relations. In Cambodia in recent times, especially all parties have tried to negotiate a mature solution.
It should be informed that this International Labor Day is organized to commemorate the implementation of the working conditions and rights and freedoms of workers and employers contained in the labor law and international conventions.
Ms. Tep Kimvannary, President of the General Confederation of Trade Unions of Cambodia (CGCU), noted that Cambodia has given full rights and freedoms to workers and employers to establish and exercise their freedom of association without coercion, intimidation or abuse. Abuse or persecution due to legal action.
Mr. Oknha Sok Piseth, President of the Federation of Employers and the Cambodian Business Association, said that the Federation of Employers will continue to help address the challenges of employers as well as encourage all employers to participate in promoting the welfare, safety and freedom of workers in accordance with National and international legal standards.
Mr. Tun Sophoan, National Coordinator of the ILO in Cambodia, stressed that in the past, in Cambodia, there was a problem with workers protesting in the streets, but now there is a change from demonstrations to sit down to negotiate. Resolve disputes in a constructive manner. Strengthening industrial relations is an important factor driving social and economic progress in Cambodia.
The ILO Representative is committed to further strengthening and expanding cooperation in partnership to continue to work for the promotion of decent work and to address common issues to protect the interests of workers, employers and all parties.
Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, said that the Royal Government has continued to promote the protection of the rights and freedoms of professional organizations, maintain harmonization of industrial relations, provide benefits to workers, including the provision of social protection in accordance with the principles. Government policy, labor law, trade union law, and the 13 international labor conventions that Cambodia has ratified.
The Minister said that the opening of the freedom of professional organizations has significantly increased the number of registered organizations in the first quarter of 2023, with a total of 6,064 registered organizations, including unions. 5,741 bases, 268 trade unions, 42 unions and 13 employers' associations.
To further improve the professional relations, the Minister recommended the following:
1. Continue to strive to implement the 10 key recommendations of the field of employment and vocational training, which were issued by Samdech Techo Prime Minister during a meeting with workers.
2. Continue to strictly implement the Labor Law, the Law on Trade Unions and international conventions aimed at strengthening industrial relations.
3-Continue to implement policies to encourage access to protection, such as access to social security, health equity fund cards, access to vocational training, employment assistance or job creation, participation in the financial system. Pensions and allowances for women at childbirth.
4-Continue to encourage and create work conditions in the ranks of workers and unions to participate in social activities to do good deeds at the local level.
5. Continue to pay attention to the work of entrepreneurs and employees of the autonomous economy of the informal economy to receive benefits in accordance with the policy of the Royal Government.
