Gross domestic product (GDP) grew at an annual rate of 1.1 percent on the back of strong consumer spending growth, Bloomberg reported on Thursday, citing a US Commerce Department initial estimate. In almost two years. US Federal Reserve inflation data hit a one-year high
The 3.7 percent increase in consumer spending reflected an increase in both goods and services, as well as an increase in vehicle purchases. Business investment in equipment fell the most since the outbreak, and declining inventories weighed heavily on two-year economic growth.
According to Bloomberg, these figures show a slowdown in economic growth due to the US Federal Reserve raising interest rates and rising inflation. Although the economy recovered at the beginning of the year helped in part by unusually hot weather, families and businesses cut back on spending as time in the first quarter progressed.
This economic outlook depends largely on the resilience of the labor market. Low unemployment and persistent wage increases have allowed consumers to overcome high inflation and continue to spend.
Russia: US-South Korea nuclear deal could destabilize region
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russia's foreign ministry has criticized the United States and South Korea over its nuclear deal, saying it would destabilize the region and the world and lead to an arms race. This is according to a quote from Reuters on Saturday, April 29, 2023.
"This development will destabilize the region and have a negative impact on regional security as well as global stability," the Russian Foreign Ministry said in a statement issued on Friday.
Moscow's criticism comes after the United States on Wednesday vowed to allow South Korea to have more say in its nuclear program in exchange for Seoul abandoning its intentions. Has its own nuclear weapon. Washington has also vowed to use nuclear weapons to defend South Korea in the event of a North Korean nuclear attack.
