(Phnom Penh): Representatives from the public and private sectors and development partners met in a consultative workshop aimed at validating the results of the assessment and to discuss recommendations for the development of sanitation markets in the northeastern region of Cambodia. April 26, 2023.
The assessment and recommendation of this assessment is to support increased sanitation coverage for people living in rural areas, environmentally vulnerable areas, especially the poor and indigenous peoples.
Cambodia is striving to achieve the Sixth Sustainable Development Goal, which ensures the availability and sustainable management of water and sanitation for all. The Royal Government of Cambodia has set a goal to achieve sustainable access to clean water and sanitation services for all Cambodians by 2025.
Although it has been noted that sanitation coverage in many provinces across Cambodia has increased in recent years, this assessment found that about 52% of the Northeast population has access to separate sanitation services. About 48 percent are still practicing open defecation.
Some of the findings include:
* Demand: People living in provincial towns and suburbs have higher demand for sanitary products and services than people in rural areas due to the availability of affordable and quality products and services, pressure from neighbors and understanding. High on the benefits of good hygiene.
People living in rural areas face more difficulties in accessing sanitary products and services, and they express a low desire for better sanitation.
For indigenous peoples, the decision to install toilets is not only hampered by access to quality and affordable products and services, but they are trusted and influenced by the elders in their community and without Experience in using toilets as well.
* Supply: * There is inequality in the supply of sanitary products and services between provincial and rural areas. Key challenges for those trying to supply sanitation products and services to rural communities include a lack of skills, mistrust between consumers and unskilled builders, long-distance transportation in difficult road conditions, and so on. This can lead to high costs and / or quality challenges.
* Management of manure by disinfection in latrines and bags and disinfection outside is still limited. In fact, sewerage services in specific locations in the province were found only in the provincial towns of Preah Vihear and Stung Treng.
Mr. Chray Pom, Technical Director General of the Ministry of Rural Development, said, "The Ministry of Rural Development has focused on developing policies, strategies and guidelines to support the rural water supply and sanitation environment. To achieve the Sixth Sustainable Development Goal and the vision of the Royal Government is that Cambodia becomes a country where all people have access to clean water and sustainable sanitation.
