Floods hit Auckland, New Zealand's largest city in January, and Cyclone Gabrielle swept across the North Island in February, leaving devastating damage. . The New Zealand Ministry of Finance estimates that about half of the damage was to public infrastructure, with more than $ 1.2 billion in damage to families and businesses.
The Prime Minister of New Zealand has said that the government will not have new taxes in the 2023 budget and that most of the repairs will be spent on reserves. "For the government, we are committed to reducing our share of spending to reduce demand in the economy," he told the Association of Employers and Manufacturers.
New Zealand is tackling historically high inflation, and the central bank has previously raised concerns that raising government spending could add to inflation. Government core spending will account for 35 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in 2022 and is projected to decline to 32 percent of GDP by 2023.
