The Indo-Pacific Economic Framework Agreement (IPEF), launched by US President Joe Biden in May last year, aims to counter China's efforts to expand its economic influence in the region, Reuters reported. .
Participating countries on the IPEF include Australia, Brunei, Fiji, India, Indonesia, Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, New Zealand, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam. Together with the United States, these countries account for about 40 percent of the world's gross domestic product.
The region already has several free trade agreements, including the China-wide Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP), which has 15 members in the Asia-Pacific region, including Australia and Japan.
"The theme of unification in IPEF focuses on economic issues and topics through which the United States and the United States will work together," Tai was quoted as saying by Reuters. "These partners in the Indo-Pacific can promote a more resilient and inclusive economy."
"In this sense, I do not see this as a conflict with other types of agreements," she said.
Tai described the US-China economic relationship as "complex" and stressed the importance of resolving these complexities and challenges, not only for the two nations but for the world.
"It will be important for us ... to recognize that as the two largest economies in the world, we have a responsibility to our own workers and to the world," she added. Also to get what is right between us.
In the sub-decree that Samdech Techo just signed on April 27, 2023, re-implemented the sub-decree dated September 25, 2012 on the establishment of the Mekong Dolphin Management and Protection Zone.
The sub-decree dated September 25, 2012 stipulates that people can fish with nets smaller than 4 cm or 4 nets in the protected area during the dry season with the owner under surveillance.
It should be noted that on February 27, 2023, Samdech Techo issued a sub-decree on the delineation of the Mekong River dolphin management area. The sub-decree prohibits all types of fishing in both protected areas and dolphin protection areas in Kratie and Stung Treng provinces.
After the sub-decree came out, the fishermen asked them to fish with small nets to get fish for food and to sell some income to support their livelihood. According to the request of the fishermen along the river, Samdech Techo decided to cancel the sub-decree on February 27, 2023 and return to the sub-decree on September 25, 2012, which allows people to spread small cells in the area. Protect dolphins from drying out.
During the opening and closing ceremonies of the Royal School of Administration on the morning of Thursday, April 27, 2023, Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that even though small nets are open, strict rules must be followed to prevent large nets fishing and Fishing uses dolphins that threaten the lives of dolphins.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen considered this decision as a consideration to ensure the interests of the people.
