General Knowledge: 8 Facts You Should Learn From Chinese Confucius Scholars
Although his ideas often reflect the ethics, family, and state leadership that are fundamental to Chinese society, most non-Chinese people know very little about his daily life and work. How well do you, the Cambodian people, know about the life and work of this Chinese scholar?
* 1: Born during a time of great political turmoil in China
In fact, the whole nation is torn to pieces by powerful people in different regions. This is why so-called Confucius was born during a time of great political turmoil in China.
Confucius' father died when Confucius was just three years old, and he was raised by his poor mother and relatives in the area. The relatives of the wrongdoers were ordinary people and their lives were very difficult at that time. In addition, his mother died before he was 40, and Confucius was only 17 at the time.
* 3: Confucius' teachings have no effect until he dies.
During Confucius' life, he was an intellectual who wandered from place to place and gave political advice to the government, guiding his ideas to people in society. In his old age, he returned home and became a low-ranking statesman.
Confucianism did not become a widespread theory until his apostles (3,000) began to sow the seeds of thought through their writings.
* 5: Confucius' teachings focus on the importance of the family
Confucius believed that it was only within the family that privacy was fully demarcated and fully developed. Thus, family duties and family relationships are the keys to his philosophy. Suppose a husband has a duty to love and give hope to his wife while the wife has to respect her husband. In the same way, you have to get respect from your younger siblings, you have to respect your parents and so on.
In addition, Confucius thought that governing a state was like governing a family, because family privacy has its roots in public life, that is, good family management creates good state governance.
* 7: Confucianism is famous and declining in the Chinese era.
The six philosophies include:
Yi: The Book of Ownership and Inheritance * The Book of Jee: A Book of Narratives for Natural Beauty * The Book of Li: A Celebration of Sacrifice Zhu: The history and genealogy of China * Zhou Xiao or Spring Book: Spring is the actual history of the Luo tribe.
According to some sources, Confucian scholars were not born in the common line, but were born in the Luo Dynasty, but gradually disappeared from Chinese history due to the war. As for the book called Zhou Xiao or the Book of Spring, it is a metaphor that Confucius recorded and described his family journey, namely the Lu Dynasty.
