Taking this opportunity, the Minister expressed his deep gratitude to the Government and people of Korea, and especially the Embassy of the Republic of Korea in Cambodia and KOICA for facilitating Cambodia to receive this precious assistance to the people of Ratanakkiri and Mondulkiri.
At the same time, Professor Mam Bunheng also thanked the local authorities at all levels, the Armed Forces of all kinds for supporting and supporting the successful implementation of the project to build a maternity and baby building.
At the same time, the Minister of Health also requested the Ambassador and the President of KOICA to continue to provide new projects to strengthen maternal and child health in these two Northeastern provinces to ensure timely services. Such as reducing the referral of patients to hospitals in Phnom Penh.
Please be informed that after the inauguration of the above building, Minister Mam Bunheng and the Korean side also visited the new maternity ward and discussed further on strengthening human resources and medical equipment. In the hospital.
Expert: Journalists are important to bring peace, stability and prosperity to the Mekong region
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of Cambodia, said that it is very important for journalists to bring factual information from the Mekong region, which contributes in some way to our Mekong region. There is peace, stability and prosperity.
Dr. Kin Phea made the remarks at the opening ceremony of the 2nd Mekong Regional Media Training Seminar on "Media Participation for Mekong River Peace and Stability". And Prosperity ”this morning, April 26, 2023, at the Royal Academy of Cambodia. The opening ceremony of the workshop was presided over by Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia.
At this point, Dr. Kin Pheap said that the governments of the six countries along the Mekong see the economic benefits of developing Mekong resources, so the six countries of the Mekong region continue to discuss, consult and share experiences. Strengthen cooperation rather than meaningless debates caused by countries outside the region to mislead public opinion.
"Journalists are very important to bring factual information from the Mekong region, which contributes to the impossibility of bringing peace, stability and prosperity to our Mekong region," said Dr. Kin Phea. .
In addition, the expert pointed out that if journalists in the Mekong region do not pay attention, the Mekong region could lead to a big problem in the political issues in the countries along the Mekong River.
According to Ky Soklim, a consultant to the Club of Cambodian Journalists, the Mekong River is a great river, so journalists in each of the Mekong countries should report as much as possible so that people living along the Mekong River Can understand each other, can love each other through cultural promotion and so on.
"What can journalists do for our entire Mekong region for peace, stability or prosperity? I think the role of journalists is very important. We can report on the potential," he said. Of the Mekong region, from culture, from food ...! ”
The Mekong region is vulnerable to manipulative tactics of democracy, human rights and the rule of law, which exacerbate geopolitical divisions, disrupt integration and cooperation, and attempts to divide the world into ideological blocks and powers. .
Democracy is being kidnapped to rule the world, the fabrication of "concerns about democracy" and the supporters of the political bloc and the anti-sectarian ideology of the Cold War.
Some countries are repeatedly calling for "democracy" and "human rights" and have used all their power to prevent countries from following their example. They always try to hide their domestic failures and chaos by shifting the blame to other countries, such as the six Mekong countries.
Democracy does not have a single set of institutions and unique features that are universally applicable. The specific form in which democracy takes place in a country must be broadly related to the existing political, social and economic situation in that country.
Democracy must not be looked at on the basis of a country's political system, ignoring the diversity or diversity of its civilization.
Please be informed that this workshop is organized by the Institute of International Relations of Cambodia of the Royal Academy of Cambodia and the Club of Cambodian Journalists. The three-day workshop will be held from April 26 to 28, 2023 at the Royal Academy of Cambodia with about 50 journalists from Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar, Thailand and Vietnam and experts from Australia.
