As India's population is expected to grow over the decades, China's population has begun to decline recently. The population declined for the first time since the 1960s after declining for six consecutive years. In the last three years, China's working population has fallen by more than 41 million, the equivalent of Germany's total labor force.
The pace of growth of the aging population is expected to be slower than in India. The South Asian nation is now one of the youngest countries in the world, with more than half of its population under the age of 30 and an average age of 28.
"Both countries should be committed to social protection and other measures to ensure the health and well-being of the aging population," the UN report said.
The two countries together make up more than a third of the world's population. This demographic change will not only pose significant economic and social challenges for both countries, but will also affect how the world achieves its sustainable development goals. According to Bloomberg News.
