The Mekong is the longest river in Southeast Asia. The river is about 4,900 km long and flows from its source on the Tibetan plateau in China through Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam through a large delta into the sea. The basin covers a total area of 795,000 square kilometers and has an average annual discharge of 475 cubic kilometers, the 10th largest in the world.
There are a number of Mekong cooperation frameworks, including: Mekong-Lanchang Cooperation (People's Republic of China), Mekong-Mekong Cooperation (India), Mekong-Japan Cooperation, Mekong-Japan Cooperation, Republic of Korea, Mekong Cooperation Krom, now a Mekong-US partnership (US) and a friend of the Lower Mekong Subregion and the Mekong River Commission.
The Mekong-US Partnership focuses on economic connectivity, human resource development, cross-border water and natural resource management, and non-traditional security, as well as emerging threats such as: health security, epidemic response, and the fight against transnational crime. Cyber Security and Anti-Trafficking in Persons, Drugs and Wildlife.
