Veterans of the Khmer Rouge: Hunting does not make the family of Thur Thea, but make life more difficult
(Pursat): A former Khmer Rouge soldier and park ranger at the Pursat Provincial Department of Environment, who used to live in the wild on wildlife, trapping wild animals for food and medicine, claims that hunting His forest did not make the family life easier, but made life more difficult.
Speaking during the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign in Veal Veng District, Mr. Sieng Sarith, a park ranger at the Pursat Provincial Department of Environment, said that after the win-win policy of Samdech Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, he remained in business. His family.
He said that his hunting did not improve the family's livelihood and that he was concerned that the forest had been cut down and burned, and that wildlife had been killed. With a love for the forest and animals, he volunteered for the Pursat Provincial Department of Environment as a volunteer.
At the same time, he decided not to eat wild animals, continue the task of removing traps and called on all people not to eat wild animals and to take care of wildlife and natural resources.
Please note that the Zero Trap Campaign in Veal Veng District, Pursat Province aims to make people aware of the dangers of traps, understand the benefits of wildlife and especially stop eating wild animals that are always harmful to health.
Through this campaign, there are more than 70 restaurants and have announced that they will not serve wild meat for guests to eat first. In particular, on social media, there are criticisms from individuals who dare to use wild meat for cooking.
