Financial and operating results are released today in the ADB 2022 Annual Report. The report summarizes how the ADB connects finance and knowledge and builds partnerships to help the region cope with the economic crisis exacerbated by Russia's invasion of Ukraine, the escalating food crisis and severe weather events.
$ 20.5 billion: loans and collateral, investment assistance and technical assistance to the government and the private sector. ADB has raised an additional $ 11.4 billion in co-financing.
ADB approves $ 6.7 billion financing for climate change mitigation and adaptation by 2022, a step towards its $ 100 billion ambition for climate financing by 2019 2030.
To address the escalating food crisis in the region, the ADB has provided $ 3.7 billion under its $ 14 billion food security program, providing essential food assistance to the most needy and strengthening the food production system. .
To support economic recovery, the ADB has provided funding for institutional reform, strengthening public service delivery, and growth in key economic sectors. ADB's decision to provide $ 3.9 billion in financing to the private sector includes:
At the same time, ADB has invested heavily in quality infrastructure as well as education, health and other social sectors that contribute to building the overall resilience of the economy.
Promoting gender equality remains a priority for ADB, with 97% of ADB operations in 2022 contributing to this. These include: Initiatives to improve women's access to quality employment, promote women's entrepreneurship and build women's resilience to climate change.
The 2022 Annual Report provides details on how the ADB is evolving to meet the changing and complex needs of its developing member states.
These include measures to gain more resources to support regional development through ongoing oversight of the capital adequacy framework as well as ADB's structural and non-structural reforms using the operational model. New.
"I am confident that these reforms will ensure that ADB provides greater impact in the region, including by expanding climate financing, mobilizing more private sector investment and providing solutions," Asakawa said. Comprehensive development to meet customer needs.
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) is committed to achieving environmental prosperity, resilience and sustainability in the Asia-Pacific region in line with its efforts to eradicate extreme poverty. Founded in 1966, the bank is governed by 68 member countries, including 49 countries from Asia and the Pacific.
