(Pursat): The Cardamom Mountains are 1.5 million hectares in southwestern Cambodia. Cardamom forests are an important natural barrier in Cambodia, as they can absorb the most carbon in Asia and reduce greenhouse gas emissions, and the Cardamom Mountains are the only last great rainforest in Cambodia. Southeast Asia.
In addition, the Cardamom Mountains are the second largest wild elephant sanctuary after the Northeast, while the Ministry of Environment, partner organizations and stakeholders are working to make the area a safe haven for Asian elephants. .
Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that currently there are between 400 and 600 Asian elephants present in Cambodia, most of which live in the Cardamom Mountains, the northern plateau and the Tonle Sap region. The eastern plateau in Mondulkiri is rich in biodiversity, ecosystems and habitat for them and other wildlife.
The spokesman said that the images obtained from the automatic cameras confirmed the presence of many Asian wild elephants walking with the herd, which was a source of hope for the conservation team. This wild elephant is growing. For the last 10 years, there has been no crime of shooting ivory elephants in the Cardamom Mountains.
In addition, Mr. Net Pheaktra continued, "In this area of the Cardamom Mountains, where Asian wild elephants live, the area we are looking at is related to the conflict between humans and elephants, which has caused many negative effects that we see suffering. Stress causes wild elephants a lot of problems with their health. When they have problems, they need to walk up to 80 to 100 kilometers when surrounded by obstacles, so they do not walk, causing health problems and mental problems. "
The project director of the Wildlife and Nature Coalition added that the project has been running for almost three years. This area is organized into two places: the protected area of Proteal Chrum village and Phchek Chrum village. Among the 10 families who received the project, it was recommended to grow crops that are not attractive to elephants, such as beans, peas, cucumbers, peppers and eggplants.
Tuy Sereyvathana said that wild elephants can be disturbed by the presence of humans in their habitat and, in addition, wild elephants can be attacked by people to the point of injury or death. Killing in retaliation for wild elephants that came to eat their crops.
