The Belarussian Defense Ministry announcement comes just weeks after Russian President Vladimir Putin said Russia would deploy tactical nuclear weapons on Belarus in response to NATO's expanding influence. Born and a warning message to this military alliance that is supplying weapons to Ukraine.
In early February, Belarus said its forces were taking control of the Iskander missile system that Russia had already provided. But later, when the unit was sent to Russia on April 4 for further training, Minsk made it clear that the training would include the maintenance and operation of a tactical warhead. Equipped on Iskander missiles.
Russia has not given a specific date for the deployment of tactical nuclear weapons on Belarus, but Putin has indicated that construction of a weapons depot could be completed by early July. This will be the first time Russia has deployed nuclear weapons abroad since the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991.
