(Pursat): Traps are hidden killers that have been threatening wildlife and biodiversity and contributing to the prevention of risks that may affect public health arising from direct contact with wildlife. The purpose of the Zero Trap campaign is to eliminate all types of traps in the forests of Cambodia.
On the morning of April 22, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, led the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign, Pursat Province, under the theme "Together we can end the crisis of trapping and wildlife trade. In Cambodia ”with the massive participation of government institutions, the private sector, civil society, students and the people to end the trap crisis in protected areas and the illegal wildlife trade in Cambodia.
Tuy Serey Vathana, Program Director of the Wildlife and Nature Coalition, said: "Stopping the habit of setting dangerous traps will bring long-term benefits to wildlife, habitats and people. "Together we protect Cambodia's great biodiversity and improve the lives of communities and public health, which makes a positive difference."
"There is not much time left for wildlife in Cambodia and we must act together now," he said. There is not much time left for wildlife in Cambodia and we must act together now to save our wildlife. "If no immediate action is taken now, Cambodia will be at risk of losing a species of wildlife of universal value that cannot be replaced for future generations."
