The European Parliament on Wednesday passed a key law that would require companies selling goods to the EU to produce "verifiable" information indicating that their goods are not grown on deforested land next year. 2020, otherwise it will face serious fines.
This rule applies to soybeans, beef, coconut oil, wood, cocoa, coffee, rubber, charcoal and processed products, including chocolate skins and furniture.
The Malaysian government has said the law is a "deliberate effort" to increase costs and constraints for its palm oil sector, a key source of Malaysia's export earnings.
Malaysia is the world's second largest oil exporter after Indonesia.
He said Malaysia was working with Indonesia to consider an appropriate response to the law, adding that Malaysia was still open to talks with the EU to address concerns over the regulation.
Malaysia has said the law will affect small farmers as they will not be able to meet the cost of complying with the law. According to the Bangkok Post.
Letter to readers: Dr. Hun Manet is a candidate for a model leader who has the ability and heart to provide a cool shade for the nation in the long run.
