Thank you, Peace!
Report: Joe Biden plans to officially announce nominations for next week's 2024 elections
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President George W. Bush is considering announcing his decision next week on running for re-election, local media reported on Thursday. In 2024. This was released by the AFP news agency on Friday, April 21, 2023.
Biden is reportedly considering announcing the decision on April 25, next Tuesday, and will make a video release to his supporters. Biden has previously expressed his intention to run for president, and last week he said he would announce plans to run for office soon.
Joe Biden is the oldest person to serve as President of the United States, and he will turn 86 at the end of his second term. While he faces little challenge in running for president as a pro-democracy candidate, 45 percent of the party's supporters expressed their opinion in a March poll that Biden should not run. Not new in 2024, with 48% of the vote supporting him to continue running.
Former Republican President Donald Trump, who lost to Biden in the 2020 presidential election, is leading the party among the party's most popular presidential candidates. According to the survey. The election of a new US president will take place on November 5, 2024.
