EU passes law on deforestation (Video inside) (Brussels): The European Parliament on Wednesday passed a law on deforestation to ban the import of coffee, beef, soybeans and other goods. EU if these goods are involved in global deforestation. According to Reuters.
According to Reuters, the law aims to eliminate deforestation from many of the daily supply chains sold in Europe. This rule applies to soybeans, beef, coconut oil, wood, cocoa, coffee, rubber, charcoal and other processed products, including animal skins, chocolate and furniture.
Deforestation accounts for about 10% of global greenhouse gas emissions that drive climate change, and the law aims to address EU contributions to the problem.
The law does not target any country, but faces reactions from those who will be among the worst affected.
Indonesia and Malaysia, the world's largest exporters of palm oil, have accused the European Union of closing the market for their palm oil. The European Union is the world's third largest importer of palm oil.
Malaysia has said it could stop exporting palm oil to the EU in response to the law, as smaller oil-producing countries have warned they could not comply with European requirements on what goods to buy. Produced when and where using precise geolocation data. According to Reuters.
China's economy grows 4.5 percent in first quarter this year (Video inside) (Beijing) In the first quarter, compared to the same period last year, as policymakers took steps to boost growth after the end of the strict Kovid eradication measures in December. According to Reuters.
Analysts expect China's gross domestic product (GDP) to grow 4 percent in the first quarter from 2.9 percent in the same period last year, analysts surveyed by Reuters. In the fourth quarter of 2022.
Recent data show that China's economy is recovering after disruptions caused by the sudden withdrawal of Kovid-19 measures in December, led by the use of services and infrastructure. But declining inflation and rising bank savings are raising questions about the strength of domestic demand.
The Chinese government has set a low growth target for this year, about 5 percent, after failing to meet its 2022 growth target. According to Reuters.
